# On the Moon, which lands first: a hammer or a feather?
subtitle: "Drop a hammer and a feather on the Moon: which lands first? Guess, then watch Apollo 15's famous test prove Galileo right."

## On the Moon, which lands first: a hammer or a feather?
affirm: playful
sources:
    - id: nasa
      title: The Apollo 15 Hammer-Feather Drop
      authors: NASA
      year: 1971
      url: https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/lunar/apollo_15_feather_drop.html
      finding: David Scott dropped a 1.32 kg aluminium hammer and a 0.03 kg falcon feather on 2 August 1971; they landed together. 'This proves that Mr. Galileo was correct in his findings.'

    path ground #8f887c points [[40,225],[150,232],[260,220],[380,228],[500,222],[620,230],[740,221],[860,229],[960,224]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    ellipse crater1 #8f887c x 180 y 200 rx 45 ry 10 hidden true
    ellipse crater2 #8f887c x 820 y 198 rx 60 ry 12 hidden true
    group moon members ["ground","crater1","crater2"] hidden true
    icon hammer size 220 set twemoji x 380 y 520 icon_look picture of hammer hidden true
    icon feather size 220 set twemoji x 620 y 520 icon_look picture of feather hidden true
    text m_h "1.3 kg" cites ["nasa"] x 170 y 520 font_size 48 hidden true
    text m_f "30 g" cites ["nasa"] x 830 y 520 font_size 48 hidden true
    text gal "Galileo was right" #2f6b8f x 500 y 590 font_size 48 hidden true

    card title "Hammer or feather: which lands first?"

On the Moon, an astronaut drops a hammer and a feather at the same moment. Which one lands first?
    draw moon hammer feather

The hammer weighs over a kilo. The feather, about thirty grams.
    draw m_h m_f

    quiz "On the Moon, which lands first?" reveal Together!
        right: They land together. With no air to hold the feather back, gravity speeds both up at exactly the same rate.
        wrong: Ask what slows a feather down on Earth.
        * The hammer
        * The feather
        + Both together

    erase m_h m_f

Astronaut David Scott tried it on Apollo 15, in 1971, live on television.
    text apollo "Apollo 15, 1971" #8f887c cites ["nasa"] x 845 y 400 font_size 40

Side by side, they hit the dust together.
    move hammer feather by [0,-185] duration 1.4 easing ease-in

    erase apollo

'How about that,' said Scott. 'Mr. Galileo was correct in his findings.'
    draw gal

Galileo had argued, more than three centuries earlier, that without air, heavy and light things fall alike.
    highlight gal

    icon earthf size 140 set twemoji x 870 y 540 icon_look picture of feather style.opacity 0.45
    text onearth "on Earth" #8f887c x 870 y 635 font_size 36

On Earth, the air pushes back on the feather's broad, light vanes, so it drifts while the hammer drops.
    move earthf path [[-35,-45],[25,-90],[-30,-135],[5,-180]] duration 5 easing linear

    erase earthf onearth

And it was a falcon's feather, a nod to their lunar module, which was called Falcon.
    text falcon "falcon feather" #8f887c x 820 y 330 font_size 42
---
